From 09a59b723b80339e9c427094bd2ba98c6496c65f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:11:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/21] video: add MediaTek MT8183 MIPI DSI host driver

Add a driver for the MT8183 MIPI DSI host controller (DSI0).  It
implements the DSI_HOST uclass: init() brings up the clock gates, the
external MIPI TX D-PHY (through the generic PHY API), the D-PHY timing
registers and the video mode timing, and enable() switches the host to
video mode and starts the stream, so that panel initialization can be
performed over the command FIFO in between.

The register map and the D-PHY timing computation are ported from the
Linux kernel driver drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_dsi.c; the video mode
timing computation (including the D-PHY turnaround adjustment taken
out of the horizontal porch) and the command queue programming follow
the ChromeOS coreboot port src/soc/mediatek/common/dsi.c, which is
proven to drive this controller on the kukui family of boards.

DSI transfers are performed through the command queue in low power
mode for panels that request it and high speed otherwise, with bus
turn-around and receive support for read commands.  The panel child
node of the DSI controller is bound from the bind() hook.

Details of the command-mode handshake, each following the proven
coreboot port:

- Bypass the MT8183 register shadow before touching the engine: writes
  only take effect on a commit event, which never arrives in command
  mode.  Without the force-commit every engine register write of a
  command transfer is deferred and each transfer stalls until its
  polls expire.

- Force command mode at the end of init, before the panel commands.
  The coreboot port never writes the mode register and relies on its
  reset default, but U-Boot runs after a firmware handoff that left
  the engine in video mode (DSI_MODE_CTRL survives the handoff and
  the core reset), where the command-queue handshake never completes:
  DSI_BUSY latches and CMD_DONE never fires.

- Clear DSI_INTSTA with a plain zero write after the idle wait: the
  flag bits clear on a write of 0 and the BUSY latch releases with
  them.  A read-modify-write that preserves DSI_BUSY re-latches the
  engine, and the read-side RACK handshake is never needed on the
  write path.

- Poll DSI_BUSY through readl_poll_timeout(), but wait for the
  completion flag in an open-coded loop: the compiled
  readl_poll_timeout() at that call site polls the register exactly
  once and returns -ETIMEDOUT immediately whatever the window.

- A transfer that still fails releases the engine through a core
  reset and resends the same command once with a five times longer
  window; a write that never completes even then is treated as
  non-fatal, since a wedged single write is less harmful than
  skipping the rest of the panel init, and the freshly reset engine
  lets the following transfers start from a clean state.  Read
  failures remain fatal.

Signed-off-by: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
